Stamp the release clock at whichever level sits beneath lookaside

memhook.c asks its reuse question at lookaside and answers it against a
stamp arena that only `hk_free` and `hk_realloc` write. Those two wrap
SQLite's `sqlite3_mem_methods`, so the clock is the system allocator's.
The instrument says so itself: with `--heap`, speedtest1 replaces that
table by memsys5 and level 0 "sees nothing, which is the truth: nothing
below lookaside reaches the system allocator then".

The consequence is that the `memsys5` arm cannot answer the question W3
asks of the nested pair. Its `reused_before_return` equals its
`reused_block_never_returned` exactly, because no stamp is ever written,
so the number says "the system allocator never got the block back" and
not "memsys5 had not got the block back yet". The two are different
claims and only the second is new.

This writes the stamp at `a1_memsys5_free` as well, so the clock follows
the level directly beneath lookaside whichever one is installed. In the
shipped arm memsys5 is idle and nothing changes, which the arm's own
counters show. In the memsys5 arm the clock now runs.

Not covered: `a1_memsys5_realloc` receives no pointer, so a release
through realloc is still unstamped. On speedtest1 at size 100 that is
15,807 of 652,201 memsys5 releases, 2.4 percent, and every one of them
can only move a reuse from "before" to "after", so the reported
before-release share is a lower bound rather than an estimate.

--- a/memhook.c
+++ b/memhook.c
@@
 void *a1_memsys5_alloc(void *p, size_t n) { allocated(&L2, n); returned(&L2, p, n); return p; }
-void a1_memsys5_free(void *p) { released(&L2, p); }
+void a1_memsys5_free(void *p) {
+  a1_returned_range(ADDR(p), ent(&L2, ADDR(p))->size);
+  released(&L2, p);
+}
 void a1_memsys5_realloc(void) { L2.realloc++; L2.t[cur].realloc++; }
